        "bookmarked": false,
        "raw": "<p>I know some people have concerns that Overcomes are used for absolutely everything that's not an Attack, Boost, or Hinder.&nbsp; Seeing it in play, I don't have an issue with it, but I wonder if anyone has used situational bonuses or penalties on them to reflect difficulty.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The book doesn't seem to speak a word about this and I've read the whole thing twice so I don't think I missed it (but I may have!).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I'm talking about a GM-imposed bonus or penalty that is not the result of some action in the scene previously (i.e., a Boost or Hinder).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>For example, if the *player* did a kickass role-play while trying to persuade the mayor, should that merit a bonus on the Overcome?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If the hero is trying to disarm the most complicated doomsday device ever made, should that incur a penalty?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If Strong Guy who regularly lifts tanks suddenly has to lift a whole mountain, does that have the exact same chance of success? Or, alternately, if the GM says he automatically picks up tanks, but he has to Overcome to lift the mountain, at what point does he need to Overcome? What if it were \"merely\" a 1000-ton chunk of debris? And is it just as easy to pick up one as the other?</p>\r\n\r\n<p>I know the answers to my own questions: \"It's not really that type of game,\" and \"You can do whatever you want in your game.\"&nbsp; But I'm wondering if anyone has encountered these kinds of situations in your games and whether you just kept to the RAW or imposed some sort of bonuses or penalties to Overcome attempts.</p>",
