      {
        "id": 230932,
        "name": "catDreaming",
        "username": "catDreaming",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/c/4491bb/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2020-06-07T17:03:52.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I do a combination of the two, really.&nbsp;</p>\n<p>For the role play example, I'd reflect the results in the narrative. A twist could be that the mayor would love to help, but there's a third party or existing issue getting in the way.</p>\n<p>For disarming a Doomsday Device, you can give&nbsp;challenges&nbsp;passive effects on the scene. For example, maybe if that hero takes a twist, it must be a major twist to reflect the added chance of things going horribly wrong.</p>\n<p>For Strong Guy, does it make sense in the narrative for him to lift an entire mountain? Comic book levels of strength vary wildly even from issue to issue, so I'd just figure out what makes sense. It could make for a cool moment for him to lift an entire mountain, as long as it fits the narrative of the story. Alternately, you could decide that it just doesn't make sense for the current narrative.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Really, I play fast and loose with the rules when it comes to soft mechanics like this. There's just one important rule to Overcomes in my opinion:</p>\n<p>Your success may be guranteed, but the consequences may vary.</p>",
        "post_number": 2,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T06:06:03.825Z",
