      {
        "id": 230933,
        "name": "Cult of Gloom",
        "username": "Cult_of_Gloom",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/cult_of_gloom/{size}/1878_2.png",
        "created_at": "2020-06-08T01:16:53.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>A RAW way to make one task stand out as harder then others is to make it a challenge that has more then one box to overcome. For example lifitng a mountain might be overcome to start to heft, then overcome to keep your footing, then overcome to get the oompf for that last push.</p>\n<p>Principles, meanwhile, are a way that certain tasks can be made easier cause they are in that characters wheelhouse. Superstrong guy has an easier time dealing with strength based challenges cause he gets to use his max die when overcoming with brute force, as an example.</p>\n<p>I would personally have no issue with saying \"hacking this computer is done at a -2 penalty because the security is that good\" as another way to handle it</p>\n<p>As a mild tangent, often times an overcome represents something that, in ideal conditions, the hero could do no problem but they are in the middle of a comic book scene. Maybe&nbsp; super strong guy can bench press a car no problem. But now he has to do it with finesse so he doesn't squish the person under the car, or they are under fire from the laser robot stormtroopers, or he has to be careful&nbsp; where he puts the car down so he doesn't squish fleeing civilians.</p>",
        "post_number": 3,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T06:06:03.904Z",
